C1M Benchmark Result - 2026. 08. 23. (일) 23:00:51 KST
Methodology changed on 2026-08-23 — see run_c1m_bench.sh header for the
full rationale.  In short: "1,000,000 simultaneously held TLS connections"
is architecturally impossible for the current thread-parking HTTPS path
(held concurrency is capped at workers x threads), so this benchmark now
measures C1M-scale CHURN: 20 h2load processes x (-c 5000 -n 50000 -r 1000
-T 30) = 100,000 concurrently held TLS connections, 1,000,000 requests,
with a watchdog that guarantees the run terminates.

RESULT OF THE 2026-08-23 RUN: STALLED (server-side defect, under
investigation).

  - Ramp-up looked healthy: ~85,000 concurrent established connections
    were reached and requests were completing.
  - Progress then stopped entirely; no h2load process ever printed a
    final table, so per-request counters are unavailable (the 0/0/0 the
    analyzer emitted means "not measured", not "all failed").
  - Postmortem state at the stall:
      * h2load-side sockets sit in ESTABLISHED with NO server-side
        counterpart visible in ss (one-sided loopback ESTAB).
      * All 12 server accept loops idle in accept(), all HTTPS pool
        workers idle in cond_wait, pool queue empty, listen Recv-Q 0.
      * Kernel counters clean: 0 ListenOverflows, 0 ListenDrops,
        0 SyncookiesSent/Failed, nf_conntrack_count ~100 / 4M max.
      * Server stayed responsive on a fresh connection throughout
        (curl 200 in ~44 ms).
  - The watchdog stopped the clients at 600 s and the server shut down
    cleanly (exit 0).  Memory envelope held: Max RSS ~86 MB.

Total Requests:     n/a (stalled before client-side accounting)
Total Succeeded:    n/a
Total Failed:       n/a
Approx Total RPS:   n/a

